@0    = ~You! You show your face here after what you did to my men?! Holger had a good hand in choosing his mercenaries, I have to give him that. It is a pity we meet under such circumstances.~
@1    = ~Welcome, welcome dear guests—Leonhard's my name, Leonhard 'the Fierce', as they like to call me. I organize excursions into the *wild*... Nothing dangerous for our customers, of course. Unfortunately, I am fully booked. So, unless you have a voucher, I am very sorry to say that you'll have to wait until the next round, when the new tours are being planned.~
@2    = ~I do have a voucher—I think. I am interested in the "special creatures".~
@3    = ~Cut the crap. I know you are the contact for the slave traders.~
@4    = ~Greetings from Holger. I was told to speak to you about an assignment.~
@5    = ~Good day to you.~
@6    = ~You! You have guts to show your face here, after what you did to my men. Well, after what you *supposedly* did to my men, as I do not have any proof that it was you who betrayed us to the Flaming Fist. I do not have much information because none of my men came out alive, but I do know that *they* appeared.~
@7    = ~You! You show your face here, after what you did to my men. Well, after what you *supposedly* did to my men, as I do not have any proof, the dead seldom tell tales.~
@8    = ~Don't pretend to be oblivious! Your men tried to enslave me. How about we "talk" about this a little, hm?~
@9    = ~They told me I shouldn't blame you. You really didn't know your bosses would try to enslave me?~
@10   = ~There you are again! What can I do for you?~
@11   = ~I have the sum you requested. Let's make the deal for Shuck's brother.~
@12   = ~I decided I don't care about Shuck's brother any more. But I want to work for you.~
@13   = ~You have your residence wall-to-wall with the Seatower commander? Isn't that a bit... bold?~
@14   = ~I'd like to work for you.~
@15   = ~Nothing. Farewell.~
@16   = ~I am here to learn more about your operations. You better participate in telling me as much as you know!~
@17   = ~No more talking! I will end your false pretenses here and now!~
@18   = ~They—ah, I understand what happened. And here I was, thinking Holger betrayed us. There was a gap in communication between me and my bosses, as it seems. Yes, coming at Holger for our losses was my idea, but seems they understood me wrong when I said that we should keep an eye on you because of your talents. I do apologize for the inconvenience, <CHARNAME>. I'd have preferred this have turned out differently, especially because then quite a lot of my best men wouldn't be dead now.~
@19   = ~So, what's it going to be? Did you come to "end" the whole affair, or can we go our separate ways like civilized people?~
@20   = ~Best you go and do not return before I change my mind.~
@21   = ~I do not judge your men. I would have done the same. You should scold them for taking on the wrong prey, though—if they wouldn't be dead already, that is.~
@22   = ~What's done is done—I certainly got the better deal on this one. But if you lay one hand on Holger, you're dead!~
@23   = ~Indeed, I came to end your presence in this tower and the city once and for all!~
@24   = ~I will take my leave, then. No more adventures into the 'wild' by Leonhard the Fierce—at least not from here.~
@25   = ~Do not worry, going after Holger is the least of my problems now. And with whom would I do so? You were very thorough in decimating my men. Nah, even my bosses got the message, you can trust me on this.~
@26   = ~Oh, you would have been a preferable business partner, I sense as much. If you ever come to Athkatla, be sure to make yourself known by our bosses. We always have use for a <PRO_MANWOMAN> like you—and I do *not* mean as wares.~
@27   = ~Oh, how blunt! And rather confrontational. Let us do this with style... yes? You are interested in the "special creatures", I take it.~
@28   = ~Fine. You have a 'special creature' in which I have a great interest. I would like to have him back.~
@29   = ~Hearing you talk like this sickens me, but I came here to free a person, so let's talk business.~
@30   = ~I will put my weapon through your skull very "elegantly" if you do not tell me where Shuck's brother is this instant!~
@31   = ~I couldn't care less about freeing a slave. I only came here because I want to work with you, nothing else.~
@32   = ~Is that so? Interesting.~
@33   = ~And you do have a voucher, indeed! Let me take this, then, before it raises false hopes in other potential customers.~
@34   = ~Is that so? Well, then I guess I do not have to delay his shipping any longer. Noted.~
@35   = ~Now then, I see you are on a quest to save a... young man in distress.~
@36   = ~So, the only person you could be interested in came to us from Nashkel, hmm? Rather skinny and not very strong, that one. Are you sure you insist on one so sickly? I have much finer specimens available, if you are willing to pay the price.~
@37   = ~I'm insisting on that one, yes.~
@38   = ~Tell me how I can get him back now!~
@39   = ~Tempting... but I guess my gold won't suffice for a "better specimen".~
@40   = ~Well then. For the modest sum of only 20,000 gold, he will be back with you by the morn'. What do you say?~
@41   = ~20,000 gold? Are you serious?~
@42   = ~Fine, here is the gold.~
@43   = ~That is a large sum, but I will be back with the gold.~
@44   = ~That's too much.~
@45   = ~Forget it, I'm out of here.~
@46   = ~I'm sorry, but that is the minimum of what I can offer. I already gave you a great discount. Look, we have to retrieve him out of the normal delivery routine, and doing so with such short notice is costly.~
@47   = ~I know, it is a surprisingly low sum, but it's a special offer for you.~
@48   = ~Hm, that would be a pity. Do return if you change your mind.~
@49   = ~Splendid! I will make preparations for the delivery while you are raising the gold.~
@50   = ~Be quick about it, as your man is being transported elsewhere soon.~
@51   = ~Wonderful! Let me stow this away carefully. I will make preparations for the delivery. Your man will be brought to the Ilmater Monastery the coming day.~
@52   = ~Oh, I am so sorry, but you are too late. The transport already took place and he is at sea on his way elsewhere. Do let me know if I can do anything else for you, though.~
@53   = ~Are there any more questions? If not, I would ask you to leave.~
@54   = ~*smiles* Well, the commander surely has his suspicions, but there is nothing he can *prove*, so I guess he thinks he's smart by letting us operate from right under his nose. He is not our customer and probably never will be, which is a pity. The man doesn't know what he is missing.~
@55   = ~You mean the commander cannot be bribed?~
@56   = ~There are people who do not get corrupted by power? I am surprised to hear that.~
@57   = ~Farewell.~
@58   = ~No, he cannot be bribed. Every time we tried—not *me*, of course—he used it to try to get a hold of us, sneaky little commander.~
@59   = ~Yes, there are wealthy men who do not fall prey to pride. The city is irritatingly lucky with both the Watch *and* the Seatower guard being under such men. Or maybe it will be to their disadvantage, as such men need to be killed to get past them, since turning them is not possible.~
@60   = ~But—no one here is planning to assassinate the commander now, is there? We, at least, have no such plans. It is so nice and comfy here in this little Balduran's Tower of his, so I would be a fool to go against him. Also, there are *so* many customers amongst the noble and wealthy who like to come to the tower, it is a pleasure to do business here.~
@61   = ~Work for me? Now, now, what could you do for me? How about getting me a cup of nice, hot tea, hm?~
@62   = ~You know what I mean.~
@63   = ~Fine, suit yourself.~
@64   = ~Do I, now?~
@65   = ~I see you before me, a paladin in shining armor, asking the slave trader for work. I am sorry if I seem hesitant.~
@66   = ~I see you before me, a person who works together with a paladin in shining armor, asking the slave trader for work. I am sorry if I seem hesitant.~
@67   = ~See, the problem is this. I do not know you, so I don't know whether I can trust you. But to get to know you, you would have to work for me, which I could only do if I knew I could trust you. You see the problem? So, the situation normally goes like this: you work for one of our partners. And if they trust you, then there might be a chance they will recommend you to us.~
@68   = ~You are working for the smugglers in Nashkel, or so I've heard. That's a good start! Do their tasks to their satisfaction, and we will hear from each other again. Until then, this will be a mere customer-seller relation.~
@69   = ~I wouldn't know of any such arrangement, so I am sorry, but this is a customer-seller relationship only.~
@70   = ~Good day to you, dear guest!~
@71   = ~Ah! So he sends you, the newly acquainted partner, hmm? Fine. We trust Holger, so if he thinks you are trustworthy, so do we. We offer you a job to protect our wares while we ready one of our shipments. Are you interested?~
@72   = ~Your "wares"? Do I get any more information than that?~
@73   = ~Just tell me what I need to know.~
@74   = ~I don't think I can help you with this.~
@75   = ~I have a suspicion about what your wares are, and I do not want any part in slave trading! Smuggling wares is fine, but not this.~
@76   = ~Why? You already know the drill from Holger. The less you know, the better—for you.~
@77   = ~You know what I stand for, so I am sure you can draw your own conclusions.~
@78   = ~But since you already asked about working for us, I am sure there will be no problems, hmm?~
@79   = ~Let's just say that we, hmm, specialize in living wares. Special creatures... very special creatures, which are sought-after in other parts of this world, where they can be acquired legally. We are only supplying the demand that is already there.~
@80   = ~But don't overstrain your brain. The less you think about it, the smoother the operation will go.~
@81   = ~We need you to be ready to protect the transfer against any unexpected visitors... like the Flaming Fist, for example. Are you in?~
@82   = ~Do you now? Hmm. A pity, you really would be strong mercenaries. Ah well. I will let you live, for Holger's sake.~
@83   = ~Oh? But you basically *begged* for it not too long ago! Well, life is full of surprises.~
@84   = ~Very good! Come to the western shore of the Chionthar, south of Wyrm's Crossing, when night has fallen. We will await you and tell you the details then. Bring your weapons, don't draw any attention, and be ready to fight any unexpected "guests"—just in case we get detected. It happens rarely, but sometimes the Flaming Fist has a lucky day.~
@85   = ~Then this is where our business comes to a quick end. Everyone—kill this fool!~
@86   = ~I will do nothing of the sort, and we both know very well where this will lead if you press it. Do you have the authority to confront me like this? I do not see any tower guards with you, so I guess the answer is "no"! Leave before this turns ugly.~
@87   = ~Hey, I have a message for <CHARNAME>. Greetings from Holger, our partners requested backup now. Go into the Seatower and talk to Leonhard 'the Fierce' on the third floor. Tell him Holger sent you.~
@88   = ~Good that you are here. The plan is the following: we Flaming Fist will approach the slavers from the south, trying to intercept the transfer of the slaves. Your part is to approach them from the north, to help us distract the slavers and prevent them from fleeing in this direction. Take down any of their mercenaries you see. Good luck.~
@89   = ~Good job on stopping the slaver mercenaries! We were able to free the slaves in the meantime and confiscate one of their ships. This was a very successful mission. Go to the Seatower commander tomorrow, as I am sure he wants to congratulate you personally.~
@90   = ~Er, boss?~
@91   = ~Ah, there's our backup. We were quite excited when Leonhard told us about heavily armed mercenaries instead of the normal smuggler rogues.~
@92   = ~All right, I'm here. Now what?~
@93   = ~I decided I'll crash your party myself. Let's see how good you are with those knives of yours!~
@94   = ~See, the wares are already boarded—except the ones we are about to acquire. I am sure you can imagine what will happen now.~
@95   = ~What? Ho, you make the decision easy, then!~
@96   = ~Don't blame our boss Leonhard the Fierce, this comes from higher up. This is nothing personal, we would have liked to do business with you. But Holger sent you, and we can't let it go unpunished that he lost the smuggler tunnels beneath the Seatower to the Flaming Fist. You understand the impact this had on our operations. Normally, we would have told Holger to meet up with us, but we'll start by collecting you for the gladiator rings of the Copper Coronet. Without you, Holger will be a sitting duck. Everyone, grab them!~
@97   = ~Ah, there's our backup. We were quite excited when Leonhard told us about heavily armed mercenaries instead of the normal smuggler rogues... What is the uproar at the ships? Flaming Fist! We've been found out! You—it must have been you! You betrayed us! Everyone, fight!~
@98   = ~Who are you? And what is the uproar at the ships? I see Flaming Fist! We've been found out! Everyone, fight!~
@99   = ~Ah, there's Holger's mercenaries. That coward didn't come himself now, did he? Yes, he's smart like that. Let you walk into the trap for him, eh?~
@100  = ~Oh, Holger was very aware and made no secret about what I should expect from you.~
@101  = ~No need for talk, I think.~
@102  = ~Don't blame our boss Leonhard the Fierce, this comes from higher up. This is nothing personal, we would have liked to do business with you. But Holger sent you, and we can't let it go unpunished that he lost the smuggler tunnels beneath the Seatower to the Flaming Fist. You understand the impact this had on our operations. It doesn't matter that the coward didn't show. We start by collecting you for the gladiator rings of the Copper Coronet. Without you, Holger will be a sitting duck. Everyone, grab them!~
